Q: dart sync* Iterable is only called when Iterable is consumed A: <p>Your <code>makeStream()</code> method is a generator. Everything that precedes <code>yield</code> is pre-initialization of the generator.<br />
Therefore, nothing happens before the generator is actually used.<br />
Therefore, early initialization (before use) will be irrelevant.</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>void main() async {
  final stream = makeStream();
  print('printing and using the stream');
  for (var v in stream) {
    print(v);
  }

  print('All done.');
  print('Try again.');
  final iterator = stream.iterator;
  print('Before first yield.');
  if (iterator.moveNext()) {
    print('After first yield.');
    print(iterator.current);
    while (iterator.moveNext()) {
      print(iterator.current);
    }
  }  
}

Iterable&lt;int&gt; makeStream() sync* {
  print('making a stream');
  for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
    yield i;
  }
}

</code></pre>
<p><strong>Output:</strong></p>
<pre><code>printing and using the stream
making a stream
0
1
2
All done.
Try again.
Before first yield.
making a stream
After first yield.
0
1
2
</code></pre>
